00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:19.190
the heck are they?

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.842
So in the most basic
vein, a notebook is--

00:03:21.842 --> 00:03:23.800
you'll see something like
this as an interface.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.130
[INAUDIBLE] This is a notebook.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.970
This is an interface
that basically gives you

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:32.450
a broken down chunk of
code and documentation

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.970
and logs that are
sent and received

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.250
from some remote
[INAUDIBLE] or local REPL.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.050
So you have some
engine that's going

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.450
to execute code or execute
documentation requests

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:44.730
and return back some display
and outcomes of what happened.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.430
So here, in this
example, you see

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:47.900
is has this really
basic notebook.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.390
It's going to import
an image, and then it's

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.460
going to render that
image in the notebook

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.860
and then print out some text.

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.390
Really super basic
stuff, but you

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:00.140
can see this is a grouping
of code and execution

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.275
in a nice, flowing manner.

00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:05.150
And you can see this as
a different interface

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.870
for notebooks but
the same technology.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:10.340
These are all Jupyter
notebook interfaces.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.120
And in particular, the benefits
of using this technology

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.050
lie around the fact that you
have code logs, documentation,

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.645
execution results all
in the same document,

00:04:20.645 --> 00:04:24.037
and that this document
is shareable to others.

00:04:24.037 --> 00:04:26.370
This means it's really useful
for iterative development.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.630
It's really handy for sharing
results with colleagues,

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.910
and it lets you integrate
various API calls

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:36.110
in one central place
that's well-formed.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:37.972
A little bit about
what's on this page.

00:04:37.972 --> 00:04:38.930
What does it represent?

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:40.340
What's it mean?

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.170
So you have things
like a status or save

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.870
indicator in the upper right, or
something about the connection

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.820
to a kernel, which is what's
actually executing your code.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.078
You have the
standard menu items.

00:04:50.078 --> 00:04:51.870
And you have things
called code cells here.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.710
So you see this thing I've
outlined is a code cell.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.140
It's a chunk of text
which represents

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.095
code in a particular
language or framework.

00:05:00.095 --> 00:05:02.720
Jupyter Notebooks in particular
are actually language agnostic,

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:03.950
though most of the
time you'll see them

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:05.325
in Python because
that's probably

00:05:05.325 --> 00:05:07.600
the most popular kernel type.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.990
And so here you see there's
some basic Python code.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:12.890
Maybe I want to load in a model
and assert that my model has

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:13.683
some version on it.

00:05:13.683 --> 00:05:16.100
And then, maybe I want to pull
something out of that model

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:16.950
and display it.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:19.700
So the second part that's
really useful in notebooks

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.700
is the ability to display
data or graphs or images

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.295
or HTML right inside
the same place

00:05:24.295 --> 00:05:26.420
where you define the code
so you don't have to jump

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.910
between systems to see results.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:30.710
The other really neat
thing about notebooks

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.482
is you can rerun just one cell.

00:05:32.482 --> 00:05:34.190
So it keeps track of
the state as they're

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:36.650
executing so you can run
everything linearly in a row,

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:37.970
like we're going to do later.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.580
Or if you are iterating
and trying to figure out

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.710
where the display you're
trying to get actually is,

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.150
you can try and look
for it, run the cell.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:45.460
If it fails, try changing it.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:46.468
Rerun the cell.

00:05:46.468 --> 00:05:48.260
You don't have to rerun
the model load that

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.530
happened in the cell before.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:51.655
It's already been loaded,
and you're just iterating

00:05:51.655 --> 00:05:54.355
on a live code session.

00:05:54.355 --> 00:05:56.480
We talked a little bit
about already the wins here,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:00.780
but mainly it's this
familiar interface

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.540
for exploring data and
exploring problems.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.800
So how's this work a bit?

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.428
So in the Jupyter
space, independent

00:06:10.428 --> 00:06:12.720
of other types of notebooks,
the Jupyter space actually

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.370
is really about
defining the protocols,

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.260
about how to communicate
between the code

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.540
execute her and the front
end or the client that

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.663
actually wants to execute this.

00:06:20.663 --> 00:06:22.080
So you'll get
something like this,

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.550
where we have some users on the
upper left, and those users--

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.370
yes, upper left for you as well.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.560
So users in the upper left.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.580
And they all will be using
some sort of Jupyter UI.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:36.330
And they have some document,
an .ipnyb file which represents

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:37.260
the notebook.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.270
And then, they're
interacting through the UI

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:43.230
with a Jupyter server which
is communicating to the code

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.410
executor called a kernel.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.660
And it basically just forwards
requests back and forth

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:48.133
through the kernel
on your behalf.

00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:49.800
And then, there's a
protocol that kernel

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.460
follows so that any client in
any different UI or interface

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.070
could do the same thing
with their own display.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:03.150
The difference between there and
other notebooks, by the way--

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.230
step back a second.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.510
Other notebooks oftentimes
bundle the kernel

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.100
and the server together.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.360
So they'll have just the
API layer to the client,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.190
and they won't emphasize
how you actually

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.980
execute, which means
it's harder and harder,

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.720
if you want to make
a new type of kernel,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.190
to just execute a
new type of language.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.690
Or if you want to
make a new UI, you

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:25.620
have to reinvent
more things here.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.370
The kernel and the UI APIs
are all independent units

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:30.470
that are all well spec.

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:32.220
So you'll see many,
many different flavors

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.880
of UI and extensions like the
ones we're talking about today.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.170
And that gives
Jupyter Notebook space

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:41.720
an advantage over many of
the other notebook options.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:44.370
So we're going to talk a little
bit about why these came about

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.672
and how come people
use notebooks.

00:07:46.672 --> 00:07:48.130
Especially if you've
never used one

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:48.900
and someone's
like, I really need

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.108
to have this notebook in
production, and you're like,

00:07:51.108 --> 00:07:51.930
what is this thing?

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:52.932
Where'd it come from?

00:07:52.932 --> 00:07:54.390
Where it came from
was this ability

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:57.850
to explore and analyze
data and find a result.

00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:00.180
So it was really handy for
data scientist workflows,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.087
where I need to load some
data that's expensive to load,

00:08:03.087 --> 00:08:05.670
and I need to iterate on a model
over and over and over again.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.950
And maybe I don't know what my
end code's going to look like.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:09.992
Maybe don't even know what
I should be doing yet.

00:08:09.992 --> 00:08:12.000
So it makes it a really
nice way to explore

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.938
without having to recompute
all the very expensive aspects

00:08:14.938 --> 00:08:15.480
of your code.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.640
And this comes into
the fact that this

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:24.640
makes some really nice
qualities for certain workflows.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.350
It also lets you record outputs.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.130
And it's also an
easy to modify tool.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:29.410
I can share this notebook
with somebody else.

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:30.650
They can reproduce
what I'm doing,

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.260
and they can modify it
slightly without having

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.640
to understand a lot of systems.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.090
I can just ship them a file, and
they have that reproducibility.

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:39.909
But there's a lot
of things that make

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:42.309
engineering teams
frustrated with trying

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:45.130
to move these from a development
cycle on a scratch pad

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.527
into a real production system.

00:08:47.527 --> 00:08:49.860
And some of these things are
around the lack of history.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.550
If you accidentally
edit a cell and save,

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:53.050
it doesn't tell you
what you edited,

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:55.990
and it's hard sometimes to go
find out what you actually did,

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.965
especially if you close your
browser or something else.

00:08:58.965 --> 00:09:01.090
They can be really difficult
to test traditionally.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:03.580
So someone will write
1,000 lines of code,

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.130
hand it over the wall, and
ask-- or 10,000 lines of code--

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:07.900
and ask, hey, make
this run in production.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:08.817
And you're like, whoa.

00:09:08.817 --> 00:09:12.340
I don't know how this works or
if it's going to work reliably.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.800
It's also a mutable document,
so you're always editing

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.110
and in place in traditional UIs.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:20.582
And it becomes hard to
collaborate on in this sense,

00:09:20.582 --> 00:09:22.540
because two people trying
to edit that document

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.623
at the same time, you can
run into a lot of issues

00:09:24.623 --> 00:09:27.320
without extensions
on the protocols.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.218
So one thing we
did at Netflix was

00:09:29.218 --> 00:09:30.510
try to fill some of these gaps.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.440
We had a ton of users
using notebooks,

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:36.940
and the things we
saw was that, instead

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.310
of trying to push those users
away from using notebooks,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.680
we said, OK, we've
got a sizable number

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.763
of users who all love
working in this environment,

00:09:43.763 --> 00:09:45.638
and they're having a
lot of friction and pain

00:09:45.638 --> 00:09:48.055
moving from that to the systems
that are already in place.

00:09:48.055 --> 00:09:49.430
So they oftentimes
had to rewrite

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:51.493
all the work they did or
had someone else rewrite

00:09:51.493 --> 00:09:54.160
all the work they did in another
place in the same exact pattern

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.960
they already solved.

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:57.670
So some things we
wanted to do is really

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:01.420
help with improving the fact
that these notebooks aren't

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.670
versions, so it's hard to
figure out what you actually

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:04.780
ran in particular time.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:05.950
They have mutable state.

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.408
And they can't
really be templated.

00:10:07.408 --> 00:10:10.112
It always requires a human
to go edit from the UI

00:10:10.112 --> 00:10:12.070
and change a variable to
rerun it, which is not

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:14.377
a very programmatic pattern.

00:10:14.377 --> 00:10:16.210
But some things we
really wanted to preserve

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.450
was results linked right
next to code, good visuals,

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:20.388
the ease of sharing.

00:10:20.388 --> 00:10:21.930
These are the
benefits, and we didn't

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:23.522
want to move away from them.

00:10:23.522 --> 00:10:25.480
So we worked on this
library in the open source

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.150
called papermill.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.813
And papermill is in this GitHub
project called nteract, which

00:10:29.813 --> 00:10:31.480
has a whole bunch of
utilities and tools

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:35.010
around notebooks, in
particular, Jupyter Notebooks.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.570
And from a high level,
it works this way.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.900
It's a program which runs like
a client, much like a UI would.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.880
And it's going to take
something as an input

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:45.610
notebook in some
path, either S3,

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.100
in Google Cloud Store
like in this example,

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:49.910
in your local file system.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.150
It's going to prioritize
and run that notebook,

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.692
and then it's going to output
it to an output location that's

00:10:54.692 --> 00:10:56.007
independent of the input.

00:10:56.007 --> 00:10:58.090
So in this case, we maybe
have four different runs

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.428
we run where we've run the
same notebook four times

00:11:00.428 --> 00:11:01.720
with a different run each time.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.180
And how would this look in code?

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.370
So it's actually a
really simple interface.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.738
You don't have to get
really complicated with it.

00:11:10.738 --> 00:11:12.530
Here, we're going to
do some examples where

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:18.590
we run papermill against a
Google Cloud papermill demo

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:19.850
with some input notebook.

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.725
And we're going to basically
output to a new place.

00:11:22.725 --> 00:11:25.100
And here, we're just running
the notebook verbatim as is.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:26.405
We say, hey, we want
to run this notebook,

00:11:26.405 --> 00:11:27.500
so what's papermill doing?

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.792
It's actually executing the
whole notebook and the end,

00:11:29.792 --> 00:11:31.940
from the beginning to
the very last cell.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:34.070
And as each cell executes,
if no errors occur,

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:35.360
it continues to the next cell.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:37.610
And as each cell executes,
it's saving the outcome

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.335
into the output location.

00:11:39.335 --> 00:11:41.210
So you get incremental
results as it's going.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.633
And it also can log out live
what's actually happening,

00:11:43.633 --> 00:11:45.050
even if you have
some interruption

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:48.485
in the middle of a cell
or some hardware dies.

00:11:48.485 --> 00:11:50.360
But if you run this
where we have some output

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.910
run of the demo and
we have demos out,

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:54.890
you see a folder in
your Google Cloud

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.560
that has these output runs maybe
are running once for each day,

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:59.513
so you put the date
into your output path.

00:11:59.513 --> 00:12:01.430
And now you actually can
see a history of what

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:02.510
you ran every single day.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:04.070
You didn't mutate the input.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:06.830
Actually, your output is
isolated from your inputs,

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:09.708
so you get some nice
immutability guarantees.

00:12:09.708 --> 00:12:11.750
You also get some version
history, in the sense--

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:13.940
if you're running things,
you can see historically

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.323
what you actually executed.

00:12:16.323 --> 00:12:17.990
But that isn't where
we stop because one

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:20.270
of the other things that we
can't do is we can't go in

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:24.080
and edit like a human and
change a parameter or a variable

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:27.002
inside the notebook and then
expect that notebook to run.

00:12:27.002 --> 00:12:28.460
So when someone
give us a notebook,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.410
we actually need the
ability to templatize it,

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:32.490
which is add parameters.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:34.430
So in this case,
you can see we're

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.430
going to run this input
notebook and pretend

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.130
this is a notebook which
goes and maybe counts

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:43.400
the number of clicks by
device in a certain region

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:46.610
for some website
you've maybe heard of.

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:49.740
The notebook that
you're running,

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.220
we would have this default
parameter cell in here

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:55.970
where you would identify--
this is my default.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:57.590
You can set the region to US.

00:12:57.590 --> 00:12:58.430
Device is a PC.

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.320
So if I were just to run this
notebook without papermill,

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.820
it would run and
say, hey, I'm going

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:04.280
to count everything in
the US that was a PC that

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:06.748
did a click on the website.

00:13:06.748 --> 00:13:09.290
But then, when we parameterize
it in this way with papermill,

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:11.750
you're going to see that we've
overwritten region devices

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.430
by inserting another code cell.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.280
So the way papermill works is
it will generate your inputs,

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:18.020
create a code cell
out of those inputs,

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:19.550
and inject that
into the notebook

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:21.635
as though if it
were a user input.

00:13:21.635 --> 00:13:23.510
And then, when it runs,
your outcome actually

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:24.960
just has that write-in as code.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:25.640
So when you read
it, there's going

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:27.380
to be the cell with a
comment saying parameters

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.870
and then all the parameters
that got injected by the system

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:30.680
on your behalf.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:38.310
And also, you don't have to
be executing this from Python.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:38.810
Excuse me.

00:13:45.227 --> 00:13:47.810
You can also execute this from
command line, which is actually

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:50.090
probably a more common
pattern, though if you're

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:52.987
trying to extend it, you want
to extend things in Python.

00:13:52.987 --> 00:13:54.820
But here, you can see
the same exact command

00:13:54.820 --> 00:13:58.288
where we did this Google Cloud
notebook input-output path.

00:13:58.288 --> 00:14:00.080
We can do the same
thing with command line.

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:01.970
We're just saying
papermill as a CLI,

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:03.380
which wraps that other call.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:05.270
And then you can
pass in parameters

00:14:05.270 --> 00:14:09.920
in any sort of JSON format
or as individual key values.

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:11.416
Technically, -y is
for YAML, so you

00:14:11.416 --> 00:14:15.870
can give it YAML as well,
though here we're passing JSON.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:17.360
So let's do maybe
what would this

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.968
actually look like if I were
calling it on the command line.

00:14:19.968 --> 00:14:21.260
So it'd be something like this.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:22.570
I run that command that we ran.

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:23.620
You're going to see
a printout of, hey,

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:25.250
I'm grabbing an input
notebook from here.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:26.208
I'm going to an output.

00:14:26.208 --> 00:14:28.160
And then you'll see
this progress bar going

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:29.690
that prints out
progress is going

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:31.445
and the rate at which
it's processing.

00:14:31.445 --> 00:14:33.320
It's configurable about
which of these things

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:35.237
you want to render by
default but it gives you

00:14:35.237 --> 00:14:38.040
some basics about what's
actually executing as it goes.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:39.120
And you could even
put it in a log mode

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.390
so that it will print out
all the logs about each cell

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:43.893
as well as saving
it into the cell.

00:14:43.893 --> 00:14:45.310
If we ran that
same outcome and we

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:48.860
want to go look and say, hey,
what's in your Google Cloud,

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:49.797
we just ran that.

00:14:49.797 --> 00:14:51.380
And actually, the
code we ran up there

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:53.930
was the exact same--
basically the exact same code.

00:14:53.930 --> 00:14:56.172
And then I point to this
papermill demo bucket,

00:14:56.172 --> 00:14:57.630
and you can see we
have the outcome

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.780
and the input separated.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:03.720
Input has 993 bytes because it
didn't have the image baked in.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:05.360
And then, the output
has 35 kilobytes

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.027
because it was that
original notebook we

00:15:07.027 --> 00:15:10.142
looked at that prints an image.

00:15:10.142 --> 00:15:11.600
So how does this
change the picture

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.400
of the interaction with
the tooling under the hood?

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.970
Like this is replacing
this whole stack?

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:17.480
Not really.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.022
It's actually just
a different client

00:15:19.022 --> 00:15:20.847
to talk to the Jupyter kernels.

00:15:20.847 --> 00:15:23.180
So it's following the same
protocols and specs as an API

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.690
client would or any other
kind of codified interface

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:28.920
you want to make or user
interface you want to make.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:31.295
And because Jupyter has these
really nice specs about how

00:15:31.295 --> 00:15:34.370
to communicate between the
REPL and the interface,

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:38.155
the papermill can act like any
of those clients and execute.

00:15:38.155 --> 00:15:39.530
So when it runs,
it will actually

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:41.390
launch a kernel
manager, which will

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:45.050
go find the kernel by name,
start it up, run all your code,

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:46.422
shut it down.

00:15:46.422 --> 00:15:47.880
And then, the only
other difference

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:49.310
is that the read and write--

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:51.602
we're reading from one source
and writing to an outcome

00:15:51.602 --> 00:15:53.997
source or outcome destination.

00:15:53.997 --> 00:15:56.330
That's a little bit different
than the maybe development

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:57.082
cycle pattern.

00:15:57.082 --> 00:15:59.540
But the end of the day, you
always have an .ipnyb file that

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:00.840
you can load in any system.

00:16:03.242 --> 00:16:05.450
If you look a little deeper
under the hood about what

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:06.980
this actually
looks like, there's

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:08.630
a few modules that go in here.

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:11.420
There's syncs and
sources, which can

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:13.860
be any kind of
schemaed destination.

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:17.740
So things like Google
Cloud, S3, Azure, File

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.400
System, [INAUDIBLE],,
those are all baked in.

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:21.650
And then, the parameters here.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:24.020
You can pass in any
kind of JSON-like value

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:25.070
and those extendables.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:27.230
So if you want to have
more customized things

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:28.610
for your platform,
it's very easy

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:31.500
to make an extension of how
the parameters get passed

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.925
in so you could do things
like, with table references,

00:16:33.925 --> 00:16:36.050
actually load the table
data on behalf of the user.

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:41.360
So the other thing this
enables is the idea

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:43.430
that you might have
a notebook template,

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:46.190
let's say something that's
a learning algorithm,

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:48.923
and you want to adjust
how you actually run it.

00:16:48.923 --> 00:16:50.840
You can run that and
parameterize the notebook

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.183
differently for each run.

00:16:52.183 --> 00:16:53.600
And then you can
run that notebook

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:57.530
and have a documented outcome
that has visualizations, logs,

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:59.930
and outcomes all in one
place for each parameter

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:01.863
variation from your
template you started with.

00:17:01.863 --> 00:17:04.280
This is really, really handy
for machine learning patterns

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:06.619
because oftentimes you want
to have a template explore

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:07.932
a little bit, find a pattern.

00:17:07.932 --> 00:17:09.349
But then you maybe
don't know what

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:11.575
parameters you should
actually use in production.

00:17:11.575 --> 00:17:13.579
So this is a way you can
fan out that notebook

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:17.990
without rewriting it along
many different parameters.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:21.250
And the end result here
is you can also then see

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:22.750
what the result of
maybe a confusion

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:25.520
matrix or some other
accuracy score on the outcome

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:27.050
is for each of those notebooks.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:33.780
So there's a few aspects
of notebook execution

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:35.160
that this extends to.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:36.750
Part of it is we've
extended the use

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.115
case to other types of users.

00:17:38.115 --> 00:17:39.990
And this is actually
where Netflix was really

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:42.600
targeting with some of
this papermill investment.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.210
Before, we just had the data
scientists had a nice iteration

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:48.039
place that didn't fit
very well into production.

00:17:48.039 --> 00:17:49.706
And now, we actually
have expanded this,

00:17:49.706 --> 00:17:51.635
so now analytics engineers
and data engineers

00:17:51.635 --> 00:17:53.010
are using notebooks
more and more

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:54.840
because they get the
benefits of logging.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:57.090
They get the benefits of
associating the thing they're

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.130
executing to outcomes.

00:17:59.130 --> 00:18:01.230
And they can
iteratively develop.

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.817
They all have an iteration cycle
that looks somewhat similar,

00:18:03.817 --> 00:18:05.650
although I would say
the data scientist this

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.325
is the most involved in
terms of iteration cycles.

00:18:08.325 --> 00:18:10.200
But now, they can develop
on those notebooks,

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:11.730
and all of those
different user profiles

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.105
can use papermills and then
schedule that on the platform

00:18:14.105 --> 00:18:18.390
or run it programmatically
at a later time for people.

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:20.080
So one example of this.

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:23.790
There was a blog post, which
is linked on the slide here,

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:28.860
around how to use papermill
with TensorFlow on Google Cloud

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:31.560
to actually execute your
notebook with GPU instances

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:33.430
or other types of
configurations.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:34.770
So here's just a little snippet.

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:36.770
I had it cut a little
short to fit in the slide,

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:38.857
but the blog has the full
code to execute this.

00:18:38.857 --> 00:18:41.190
But you're basically just
going to run your Google Cloud

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:43.870
compute instance create
call with an instance,

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:46.913
and then you're going to pass
in your GPU count and type.

00:18:46.913 --> 00:18:48.580
And then, you're also
going to say, hey,

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:50.670
my startup script is this
papermill input path,

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:51.260
output path.

00:18:51.260 --> 00:18:53.760
And you can imagine adding any
kind of parameterization here

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:55.240
in that you would want.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:58.245
And then, you can tell Google
Cloud to delete that instance

00:18:58.245 --> 00:18:58.870
when it's done.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:01.560
And this basically has
now made a orchestration

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:06.298
for executing a notebook on
top of another set of hardware,

00:19:06.298 --> 00:19:08.340
including hardware that
you might not have access

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:09.356
to in your local dev.

00:19:12.212 --> 00:19:14.308
The other thing too is
this all open source code,

00:19:14.308 --> 00:19:16.350
so all the things I'm
showing aren't proprietary,

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:17.250
hidden behind walls.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:21.390
The Google Cloud
storage was actually

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:24.090
merged into the open source,
so it's very easy for anyone

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:26.410
to access with their
tooling in the Google Cloud.

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:28.800
S3 and Azure are
of similar stories.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:31.210
And it's really easily
accessible to any other scheme

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:32.430
we want to add.

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:35.730
We'll talk about how
they do that in a minute.

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.497
And the whole papermill repo--

00:19:38.497 --> 00:19:40.830
which, by the way, had the
1.0 release actually went out

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:42.720
today, which was
feature completeness

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:45.600
and all the little things
upstream finally got

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:48.120
finished and merged
into releases.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:52.320
So it's a really nice,
clean repo to read now.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:53.890
But all of it is plug and play.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:56.430
So how you read from
syncs and sources,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.640
how you actually
execute the notebook,

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.440
and how you apply
parameters are all

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:04.320
registered classes that you
can register your own custom

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.270
versions of.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:09.990
Say, for example, we want to
implement an SFTP handler.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:12.600
Say we have some files
in SFTP because that's

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.010
where someone has
some notebooks,

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:15.760
or they want their
notebooks to land there

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:19.080
because they need to share
across some B2B channeled that

00:20:19.080 --> 00:20:21.365
still is using SFTP.

00:20:21.365 --> 00:20:22.740
To implement this
would literally

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:23.790
be all the code on here.

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.390
If you implemented
read and write

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:27.975
and then did the
setup tools here,

00:20:27.975 --> 00:20:29.850
this would be all the
code to be able to call

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:31.725
the command at the
bottom, which is papermill

00:20:31.725 --> 00:20:34.690
execute with an SFTP scheme.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:37.220
It has this entry points
pattern you could do,

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:39.360
where you can register
in your own setup pie

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:44.070
an entry point about
how to register an IO

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:46.020
interface in and for
the other interfaces

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:47.880
of a similar pattern.

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:49.380
So the code actually
execute things.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:51.300
If you read the whole repo,
it's really short now.

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:52.900
There's not very
much code in there.

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:55.500
So it's pretty easy to
grok what it's doing

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:56.550
and to add new things.

00:20:59.435 --> 00:21:00.810
So one thing I
want to talk about

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:02.477
is actually the failure
mode is actually

00:21:02.477 --> 00:21:04.200
really nice in notebooks.

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:05.850
Traditionally, if
you think of, how

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:08.550
would I have a bunch of scripts
that maybe I didn't quite

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:10.650
productionize and
put into the library,

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:13.890
or maybe it's calling things
across three different systems,

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:17.980
how would I actually understand
what went wrong when it fails?

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.077
And this is what keeps
people like me employed

00:21:20.077 --> 00:21:21.660
because it's a hard
problem, so we can

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:23.970
go build solutions for people.

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:27.120
But one of the nice
things is with a notebook,

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:28.915
if you failed with
a notebook and we

00:21:28.915 --> 00:21:30.540
call that same pattern
where we fan out

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:33.420
over some parameter,
the failed notebook

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:36.010
actually has a really rich
source of what happened,

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.290
how'd it go wrong.

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:39.303
how do I reproduce the problem.

00:21:39.303 --> 00:21:41.220
You get all of that
captured in that notebook.

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:43.053
Because it failed, it'll
actually still save

00:21:43.053 --> 00:21:44.360
the outcome, but it'll save it.

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:45.210
Here's the stack trace.

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:46.330
Here's the cell it failed.

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:48.000
Here's how the code
didn't succeed.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:49.890
And your parameters, when
you're running the papermill,

00:21:49.890 --> 00:21:51.210
are already baked
into that notebook,

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.877
so you don't have to
re-parameterize it.

00:21:52.877 --> 00:21:55.560
It will run exactly as it
ran when you were scheduled

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:58.900
or running inside a platform.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:01.347
So here, for example,
we have this notebook.

00:22:01.347 --> 00:22:03.180
These are some of the
advantages that I just

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:05.763
talked about with stack traces
and rerun-ability and execution

00:22:05.763 --> 00:22:07.993
logs.

00:22:07.993 --> 00:22:09.660
But let's say that
notebook we just had,

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:11.783
we failed and it crashed.

00:22:11.783 --> 00:22:13.200
Well, something
we can go do is go

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.492
find the issue in the notebook.

00:22:14.492 --> 00:22:16.920
Say here we found this
cell execution number 12--

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:18.550
I've cut out middles layers--

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:22.860
has some sort of HTTP connection
failure talking to Spark.

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:26.880
We can go load that notebook
into a notebook dev,

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.620
iterate on it until
we fix the problem

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:30.120
and identify what's
actually wrong.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:31.710
And then we can
ship that fix back

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:33.330
to the upstream up notebook.

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:34.890
In this case, for
example, we had

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:38.273
a Spark job is defaulting to
a hostname that didn't exist.

00:22:38.273 --> 00:22:39.690
So it's trying to
target some YARN

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:40.900
cluster that doesn't exist.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.560
So maybe we need to tell it
where the YARN cluster actually

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:44.170
is.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:45.350
And now the job
actually succeeds,

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:46.710
and we can push that change up.

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:48.168
But we reproduce
the exact problem,

00:22:48.168 --> 00:22:52.350
and we didn't have to go and ask
five systems what went wrong.

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:57.810
So what these very simple change
to how you execute notebook

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.810
with a client that isolates
the inputs and outputs gives

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:03.152
you is that you get immutable
inputs if you're not

00:23:03.152 --> 00:23:04.860
changing your input
that you've scheduled

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.350
or are executing on
a programmatic basis.

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:08.980
You get immutable
outputs if you're

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:10.480
saving your output
to a unique path,

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:13.740
for example, we put to
whatever the user's execution

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:15.600
context plus a GUID
for every single run,

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:17.153
and then we associate that back.

00:23:17.153 --> 00:23:18.570
And we put it into
someplace where

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:22.790
it's read only so users don't
have access to go edit it.

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:24.930
That means you have very
good reproducibility

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:28.690
and audit history about
what actually executed.

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:30.690
You also get nice
parameterization notebook run.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.990
So no longer do you have to
have a big block of instructions

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:36.060
for the human to go
change a notebook,

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:39.120
like, when running this, change
these three lines in this cell,

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.320
and then go change these
four lines in this cell.

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:42.490
You don't do that anymore.

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:44.282
You can just parameterize
and templatize it

00:23:44.282 --> 00:23:46.097
so the user can just
pass in parameters.

00:23:46.097 --> 00:23:48.180
And then, the other thing
too is the configuration

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.180
of sourcing and syncing.

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:51.417
This means that you
can get interface right

00:23:51.417 --> 00:23:53.125
into the platforms we
already share files

00:23:53.125 --> 00:23:55.590
or where you already
have hosted systems

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:57.840
without having to
rewrite the execution

00:23:57.840 --> 00:23:59.910
and wrap it in your own
copy and move and shift.

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:06.450
So given all that, it also makes
a pretty compelling argument

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:08.760
for being able to
test notebooks better

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:10.640
with papermill as
opposed to before,

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:12.390
testing it was usually
ship it to somebody

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:15.060
and have them run it and
say, yeah, this is good,

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:18.330
and hope they were right.

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:19.955
So in terms of
notebooks, one thing

00:24:19.955 --> 00:24:22.080
to note when you're talking
about testing notebooks

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:24.660
is notebooks make pretty
terrible libraries.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:26.350
They make great
integration tools.

00:24:26.350 --> 00:24:29.520
But if you have a ton of
library code in there,

00:24:29.520 --> 00:24:32.250
they're pretty hard to make
reliable and know that you

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.230
can share and make it reuse.

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:36.550
I'll talk about what that means.

00:24:36.550 --> 00:24:38.242
So they make a good
integration tool

00:24:38.242 --> 00:24:40.200
because notebooks are
really good at connecting

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:43.290
different pieces of technology,
logging how it actually ran,

00:24:43.290 --> 00:24:45.810
building a result, or
taking some actions

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:47.880
on disparate pieces
of technology

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:51.480
without having to
leave an ecosystem.

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:54.210
But they're unreliable
when they're really complex

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:56.260
or they have a really
high branching factor.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:25:00.330
So when you have a notebook
that has 15 conditionals inside

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:02.538
of it or many different
functions and loops,

00:25:02.538 --> 00:25:04.080
it can be really
hard to reason about

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:05.288
if this thing's going to run.

00:25:05.288 --> 00:25:07.080
The traditional
path areas with code

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:08.997
is you would go unit
test each of those pieces

00:25:08.997 --> 00:25:11.430
and break them up.

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:14.790
With notebooks, since unit
testing is a bit hard,

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.768
we actually, at
least at Netflix,

00:25:16.768 --> 00:25:18.810
have adopted some of these
development guidelines

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:20.790
for the more critical things.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:22.470
This is keeping a
low branching factor.

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:24.330
Short and simple is better.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:25.800
Try to keep to one
primary outcome.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:27.240
So if you have a notebook
which does five things,

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:29.310
maybe consider breaking
that into five notebooks

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:33.300
and have five notebooks that
do one, each doing its own job.

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:35.200
And try to-- I put
this in parentheses

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.500
because we fail at it too--
leave library functions

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:38.063
in libraries.

00:25:38.063 --> 00:25:40.480
So if you get a really complex
notebook and it's important

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:42.438
and you want to move
production, move that code

00:25:42.438 --> 00:25:45.180
into a library that's shared
across your notebooks.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.190
Maybe talk to your--
whoever's helping

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:51.255
support you to make that
happen if you don't know how.

00:25:51.255 --> 00:25:53.130
But we do still get an
advantage of something

00:25:53.130 --> 00:25:56.020
we couldn't do before, which
is integration testing.

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:57.870
So if you want to write
an integration test,

00:25:57.870 --> 00:26:00.370
you basically just want to write
exactly what the user would

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:02.410
have run, a template
call into your notebook.

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:03.930
So say we have
that Spark template

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.040
that we showed failing earlier.

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:06.090
We want to write
an integration test

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:07.530
because it failed for the
user because they didn't

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:08.838
have a cluster host to find.

00:26:08.838 --> 00:26:10.380
And we could have
easily caught that,

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:13.060
that the default value
was going to fail.

00:26:13.060 --> 00:26:16.350
Here what we can do is run
that same Spark template,

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:18.420
and then we're going to
do some test run output.

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:22.560
Maybe we would automate this and
have a test run idea in there.

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.440
And then, what
we're going to do is

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:27.600
pass in a fake region, a fake
run date, and the debug flag

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:28.140
just in--

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:31.530
for notebooks that want
to be able to maybe dry

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:34.452
run their execution if
they're in debug mode.

00:26:34.452 --> 00:26:37.035
And in this case, we're going
to be targeting this region that

00:26:37.035 --> 00:26:41.255
has tons of users called luna
We're going to pick a run date.

00:26:41.255 --> 00:26:42.630
Maybe that doesn't
really matter.

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:45.060
We'll fix it to
a hard-coded day.

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:46.770
Really-- is everyone
awake, by the way?

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:48.228
I've just talked
about, like, cool,

00:26:48.228 --> 00:26:50.750
finding people on the
moon, and no one even--

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.670
OK, cool.

00:26:53.670 --> 00:26:56.070
So we've got the--

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:58.473
here are parameters
that got passed in.

00:26:58.473 --> 00:26:59.640
You see region got assigned.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.890
The run date got assigned,
and our debug true.

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:04.590
And then, here, this Spark SQL
that we're actually running,

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:08.110
maybe it's running a particular
query against an output table.

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:11.400
And now, using our parameters,
we can reorient how this runs

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:12.893
and run it against dummy data.

00:27:12.893 --> 00:27:14.310
So we'll do this
a lot where we'll

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:15.840
run integration tests
at Netflix, where

00:27:15.840 --> 00:27:17.010
we'll run all the templates.

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:19.470
And we have default
tables with some

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:22.830
known potentially problematic
patterns or just the simplest

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:23.712
thing we can make.

00:27:23.712 --> 00:27:25.170
And then, we run
the same templates

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:27.128
the users are going to
use in the same way they

00:27:27.128 --> 00:27:31.570
would use by targeting that
table or that piece of code.

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:34.050
And this gives you
a really nice way

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:36.250
to say, hey-- and this
notebook runs well.

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:37.750
I know all the
defaults makes sense,

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:40.620
and I know I can
parameterize it in some way.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:42.562
You could also, if you
have a DAG execution,

00:27:42.562 --> 00:27:44.520
you can do things like
run the integration test

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:47.140
and then have a follow up
unit in your DAG that says,

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:49.423
hey, did this actually write
data if you're concerned

00:27:49.423 --> 00:27:50.715
about that, and you can branch.

00:27:50.715 --> 00:27:52.980
It gives right into the
wheelhouse of integration

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:54.720
testing if you've
ever dived into how

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:56.310
QA thinks about testing.

00:27:56.310 --> 00:27:57.930
It gives a nice
breadth of coverage

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.585
for notebooks in that space.

00:27:59.585 --> 00:28:01.710
So that means is what are
some guidelines about how

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:03.330
I test my notebook?

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:04.940
Unit testing your
notebook is hard.

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.450
The tooling out
there isn't great.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:09.510
Or even if you had good tooling,
it's a little bit cumbersome

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:11.970
because how do you-- you had
this one document you could

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:13.290
bundle and send to somebody.

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:15.510
But now, how do you
bundle it with unit tests?

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.225
Do you put them in your notebook
and make it really cluttered?

00:28:17.225 --> 00:28:18.450
Do you put it alongside?

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:20.970
Then, why don't you just
make a library anyway?

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:22.783
There are some hard
conversations there.

00:28:22.783 --> 00:28:24.700
So what we actually ended
up doing was saying,

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:26.742
hey, use notebooks throughout
the good app, which

00:28:26.742 --> 00:28:28.800
is integrations or
reproducibility,

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:31.470
and try to follow these
guidelines to make sure

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.990
that you can trust that
they're going to work.

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:36.780
If you have a simple
one-pager of a notebook,

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:38.870
one integration test
is probably sufficient.

00:28:38.870 --> 00:28:39.930
You'll be fine.

00:28:39.930 --> 00:28:42.930
If you have something that
has maybe a little complexity,

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:46.020
maybe it's two pages
of code on your screen,

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:47.970
maybe a couple integration
tests for a few

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:49.970
of the patterns that could
go through it to make

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:53.070
sure you cover more of the lines
usually covers you pretty well.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:55.740
And you'll be pretty good
for production use cases.

00:28:55.740 --> 00:28:57.360
If you do a really
complex notebook--

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:01.170
like I wrote my whole ML
training, loading, saving,

00:29:01.170 --> 00:29:03.390
and three different model
iterations in the same

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:06.210
notebook, and it's like
a 20-page document--

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:09.210
one, maybe don't
put that in prod.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:12.150
If it's critical, move some
of that code into a library

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:15.030
so that the shared code
between your model execution

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:16.530
can be loaded in
all your notebooks.

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:17.880
And then your notebooks
get nice and trim,

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.088
and it's more of an integration
where your library is

00:29:20.088 --> 00:29:22.000
one of the integration pieces.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:24.510
And then, if you can't
do that and you still

00:29:24.510 --> 00:29:27.000
need to get it running,
trying to get one integration

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:29.340
test per usage pattern of how
this will be used at least

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:30.750
gives you some confidence.

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:34.257
It's not a silver bullet,
but it gets you further.

00:29:34.257 --> 00:29:36.090
And one thing I want
to really emphasize too

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:37.440
is that I've talked
a lot about papermill

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.410
and how you can use this
tool to change notebooks.

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:41.785
There's actually a ton of
other goodies in the ecosystem.

00:29:41.785 --> 00:29:44.160
Because it's all spec
and protocol based,

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:46.680
notebooks actually have
a whole rich environment

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:49.560
of libraries that give you
different capabilities.

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:51.015
And oftentimes,
in today's world,

00:29:51.015 --> 00:29:52.890
a lot of notebook teams
that are being formed

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:54.765
are really about how to
collect these goodies

00:29:54.765 --> 00:29:57.540
and put them into a platform
that works for your use cases.

00:29:57.540 --> 00:30:00.630
So a lot of tools that you
probably haven't heard of

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:02.430
but are used by platform
teams all around

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:04.020
that I would encourage
to look into.

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:06.450
nbconvert is something that
actually papermill uses,

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:08.250
but it can have
other export forms.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:11.780
So you can make HTML or PDF
outcomes of your notebook.

00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:13.250
commuter is a
read-only interface

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:15.660
for notebooks, probably the
most useful out of this list

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:17.910
because it makes sharing a
lot safer and a lot easier.

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:20.160
You don't have to have a
whole notebook server up just

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:21.780
to share the results
of an execution

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:24.536
or of how you're
planning to do something.

00:30:24.536 --> 00:30:27.810
And there's a few others
in here, some of which

00:30:27.810 --> 00:30:30.720
I'll talk about for
a couple minutes.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:32.670
So scrapbook is one of them.

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:35.730
Scrapbook is another
repo that I've

00:30:35.730 --> 00:30:37.842
written from scratch
that basically pulled

00:30:37.842 --> 00:30:40.050
some functionality that was
in the original papermill

00:30:40.050 --> 00:30:43.710
implementation and put
it in its own bucket.

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:47.070
And what this is really
trying to do is, as a tool,

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:49.920
Scrapbook is intended
to complete the story

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:52.660
arc of notebooks as a function.

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:54.870
So with papermill now, you
have an input notebook,

00:30:54.870 --> 00:30:56.820
and you have papermill.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:59.070
So you have the function
you're actually going to run,

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:01.230
and you have parameters
as the inputs.

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.210
But your outcome today
is still a notebook.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:05.040
So when you outcome a
notebook, it's still

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:08.790
mostly human interpretable,
not machine interpretable.

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:10.980
One of the things that
this adds is the ability

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:14.180
to save results by name in your
notebook and then recall them.

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:15.930
And what this is really
useful for-- say I

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:17.513
ran that notebook
that was an ML model

00:31:17.513 --> 00:31:19.327
and I want to iterate
all my parameters.

00:31:19.327 --> 00:31:20.910
One of the things I
might want to save

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:22.548
is the confusion matrix result.

00:31:22.548 --> 00:31:24.840
And then I want to collect
all those confusing matrices

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:26.900
and find the parameterization
that was best

00:31:26.900 --> 00:31:28.955
or fit some criteria.

00:31:28.955 --> 00:31:31.080
This lets you do that pretty
easily without leaving

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:32.430
the notebook.

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.650
I encourage you to take
a look at the GitHub

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:37.090
to look into more details
about how that works.

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:39.090
But here in this example,
inside the notebook

00:31:39.090 --> 00:31:41.150
in the first section,
we're importing scrapbook

00:31:41.150 --> 00:31:42.900
and gluing our model results.

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:45.060
And then, later, outside
the notebook, maybe

00:31:45.060 --> 00:31:47.040
after we've executed it
or in another notebook,

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:48.748
we're going to read
that outcome notebook

00:31:48.748 --> 00:31:52.170
and then go collect the
model result by key.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:54.790
And you can also save things
like graphs and recall them.

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:55.860
So one of the things
that's really handy

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.230
is if you have a performance
graph, you can actually

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:01.570
save performance graph alongside
your confusion matrix result.

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:03.570
And then, when you find
the confusion matrix you

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:07.030
like the most, you can actually
go render the graph for that

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:09.488
run as well and not
have to re-execute it.

00:32:09.488 --> 00:32:10.530
It's a handy little tool.

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:11.113
Take a look.

00:32:11.113 --> 00:32:13.530
It's a little bit early in the
sense of where it could go,

00:32:13.530 --> 00:32:15.572
but it has full feature
capability for the things

00:32:15.572 --> 00:32:17.808
I just described.

00:32:17.808 --> 00:32:19.350
And I talked a little
about commuter.

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:21.430
Really highly recommend
looking into this one.

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:22.888
This is the interface
for commuter.

00:32:22.888 --> 00:32:25.530
This is our Presto template
for how to execute Presto jobs.

00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:26.970
It also fits on one page.

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:27.930
It's nice and clean.

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:29.370
There's almost
nothing happening.

00:32:29.370 --> 00:32:31.890
But it's really handy
because these calls here,

00:32:31.890 --> 00:32:35.700
we have schemaed input, and
then we have a gaze here

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:37.650
that prints out the
job information, then

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:40.080
a monitor at the end.

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:42.430
Here, we can share this
notebook template with users,

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:43.410
and when they actually
execute, they'll

00:32:43.410 --> 00:32:45.420
see this but parameterized
with their logs in there.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:47.130
And we always share through
commuter to the user

00:32:47.130 --> 00:32:48.900
so they have a really
nice way of seeing

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:51.180
what happened without the
risk of editing anything.

00:32:53.180 --> 00:32:54.930
So I've talked a lot
about the open source

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.120
and hinted about
how we use it a lot.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:00.812
One of the things at Netflix,
in about a year and a half

00:33:00.812 --> 00:33:02.520
ago when we kind of
started really diving

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:05.160
into this adventure,
there was a strategic bet

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:07.665
made that the large
swath of users

00:33:07.665 --> 00:33:09.540
that we have that we
were hiring the most of,

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:11.760
which were analysts,
were actually

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.040
using notebooks
instead of other tools

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:16.290
that us leaning into
making notebooks easier

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:18.450
would have benefit the company.

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:20.640
And then we took it
a little further,

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:23.430
almost to crazy levels, where
we were doing a new scheduler

00:33:23.430 --> 00:33:24.210
project.

00:33:24.210 --> 00:33:26.400
We decided to make every
single scheduled job be

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:30.870
a notebook, which
might sound extreme,

00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:33.060
but actually, it
played out very well.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:35.280
Because what we really
did was we made templates.

00:33:35.280 --> 00:33:37.200
And the users didn't even
know they were using notebooks

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:37.700
at first.

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:39.390
They say, I'm
running a Spark job.

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:41.145
I'm running a
transport to Druid.

00:33:41.145 --> 00:33:43.020
They're just giving the
inputs and parameters

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:46.260
for that type of job that
schemaed like any other job

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:46.950
definition.

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:48.325
But the end of
the day, we always

00:33:48.325 --> 00:33:49.740
translate that to a notebook.

00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:53.872
So it's really easy for
anyone to debug what happened.

00:33:53.872 --> 00:33:55.080
I'll even put this the point.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:57.580
We had a manager of a team who
hadn't written code in a long

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:59.240
time, and his on-call person--

00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:01.560
this is a machine learning team.

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:04.890
His on-call was out sick, and
one of their critical jobs

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:05.660
failed.

00:34:05.660 --> 00:34:06.830
And he told me afterwards--

00:34:06.830 --> 00:34:08.969
I didn't even know he did
this-- but he said, oh yeah,

00:34:08.969 --> 00:34:10.230
I got paged because he was sick.

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:11.310
And I went and
looked at the job,

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:12.580
and it had this notebook thing.

00:34:12.580 --> 00:34:13.139
I don't know what it was.

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:15.060
I clicked on it, and it had
this really nice visual,

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:17.130
and then it told me exactly
what the stack trace error was.

00:34:17.130 --> 00:34:19.088
And I read it, and I
understood what was wrong,

00:34:19.088 --> 00:34:22.050
and I actually fixed it and then
reran the job, and it worked.

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:24.909
And that was a manager of
a team was able to do that.

00:34:24.909 --> 00:34:26.489
It lowers the
barrier quite a bit

00:34:26.489 --> 00:34:29.760
on understanding what actually
happened and what went wrong.

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:30.873
So that's a fun thing.

00:34:30.873 --> 00:34:32.790
And since then, since
we started this project,

00:34:32.790 --> 00:34:35.830
we have moved like
over 10,000 jobs.

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:39.060
It produced like on the
order of 150,000 queries

00:34:39.060 --> 00:34:41.675
a day to all be running in
this notebook framework.

00:34:41.675 --> 00:34:43.050
And we've had
pretty good success

00:34:43.050 --> 00:34:46.264
being able to support that.

00:34:46.264 --> 00:34:47.032
Cool.

00:34:47.032 --> 00:34:48.699
That's what I have
for the presentation,

00:34:48.699 --> 00:34:51.159
and then I think we have
some time for questions.

00:34:51.159 --> 00:34:53.022
But yeah.

00:34:53.022 --> 00:34:54.966
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:06.390
AUDIENCE: I would like to
ask about that performance

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:09.330
and potential impact
of the performance

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:13.170
compared to running a
clear Python [INAUDIBLE]??

00:35:13.170 --> 00:35:15.198
Is there any difference, or--

00:35:15.198 --> 00:35:15.990
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:17.520
So the question was,
is there any difference

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:19.860
between running pure Python
and running a Python notebook?

00:35:19.860 --> 00:35:20.670
AUDIENCE: Exactly.

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:22.890
MATTHEW SEAL: The
answer's pretty much no.

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:24.708
In reality, this is all--

00:35:24.708 --> 00:35:27.000
the only thing you have is
a little bit of startup time

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:28.610
in order to launch
the kernel, which

00:35:28.610 --> 00:35:31.600
is on the order of
a second or two.

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:33.600
And then, after that,
you're running Python code

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:36.900
with IPython the same way you
would run Python code normally.

00:35:36.900 --> 00:35:40.390
So there's no real
performance impact.

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:40.890
Yeah?

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:43.950
AUDIENCE: Does the kernel
had a built-in compiler

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:48.168
or interpreter, or is it using
the system's interpreters?

00:35:48.168 --> 00:35:50.210
MATTHEW SEAL: The kernel's
responsible for making

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:51.525
sure it can execute the code.

00:35:51.525 --> 00:35:53.275
So in most case-- like
in the Python case,

00:35:53.275 --> 00:35:55.840
it's wrapping IPython, so
it's launching a process--

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:56.840
AUDIENCE: It's built in.

00:35:56.840 --> 00:35:57.320
MATTHEW SEAL: It's built in.

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:59.330
AUDIENCE: So if you
want to make sure you're

00:35:59.330 --> 00:36:02.300
compatible with a
server's version,

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:03.830
you have to do something else.

00:36:03.830 --> 00:36:04.000
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:05.810
Oftentimes, people
for version things,

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:07.760
like Python or Scala
or some other--

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:09.170
or R if you're using it--

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:11.480
they'll name their
kernels with the version.

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:16.370
So you'll say, I am an
IPython version 3.7 kernel.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.230
So you know you're
running Python 3.7.

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:19.500
That's the standard
you're executing against.

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:21.250
It's much like you
would say, hey, execute

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:23.380
this job with Python 3.7.

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:25.100
Same way with Scala
and others and Spark.

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:26.240
They'll oftentimes
put the version

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:28.460
in the metadata of the
kernel so it's very clear

00:36:28.460 --> 00:36:30.440
what you're executing against.

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:33.030
How it chooses the kernel is
actually inside the notebook,

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:36.020
it saves when you run it
or you connect to kernel.

00:36:36.020 --> 00:36:39.230
It saves in the metadata of the
notebook, I ran on this kernel.

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:41.502
When it runs, it tries to
find exactly that kernel,

00:36:41.502 --> 00:36:43.460
and then it has fallback
mechanisms if it can't

00:36:43.460 --> 00:36:46.090
to try best effort if you want.

00:36:46.090 --> 00:36:47.440
AUDIENCE: So before--

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:48.540
MATTHEW SEAL: Oh, sorry.

00:36:48.540 --> 00:36:49.290
You, and then you.

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:52.010
AUDIENCE: So before
notebooks came to Netflix.

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:53.440
So what was the
other alternative

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:54.810
for doing the same thing?

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:55.170
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:56.868
So what is the
alternative to doing what

00:36:56.868 --> 00:36:58.160
we're doing with notebooks now?

00:36:58.160 --> 00:36:59.952
Well, one is we had a
really old scheduler,

00:36:59.952 --> 00:37:02.030
and it was a real
pain to debug it.

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:02.980
There were like two
guys who could do it,

00:37:02.980 --> 00:37:05.022
and everyone else was
like, hey, this went wrong.

00:37:05.022 --> 00:37:07.200
And those two guys got
really, really busy.

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:09.170
So on the scheduling
side, the story

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:12.387
was kind of unscalable
for what we had.

00:37:12.387 --> 00:37:13.970
We could have solved
it in other ways.

00:37:13.970 --> 00:37:17.540
On the iterative cycle
and moving things

00:37:17.540 --> 00:37:20.030
the prod side of things
with notebooks, the story

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:21.825
was the story that's
a lot of places today,

00:37:21.825 --> 00:37:23.450
which is every time
you wrote something

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:25.220
in notebook, after
you got done, you

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:27.832
got told to go rewrite
that someplace else.

00:37:27.832 --> 00:37:29.540
And sometimes you did
a good job of that,

00:37:29.540 --> 00:37:32.000
and sometimes you threw it over
the wall to data engineering.

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:34.458
And sometimes they did it, and
sometimes they threw it back

00:37:34.458 --> 00:37:34.980
at you.

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:36.650
So there was a lot of friction.

00:37:36.650 --> 00:37:38.487
And it was OK and
at a certain scale,

00:37:38.487 --> 00:37:40.320
but we were clearly
seeing the warning signs

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:42.425
that the user base
that's using this

00:37:42.425 --> 00:37:45.020
is growing way faster than
all the other user bases.

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:47.180
So rather than running
away from the technology

00:37:47.180 --> 00:37:48.700
they like and use,
we wanted to move

00:37:48.700 --> 00:37:51.200
the platform closer to them so
that there was less friction.

00:37:55.450 --> 00:37:56.200
At the back there.

00:37:56.200 --> 00:37:56.700
Sorry.

00:37:56.700 --> 00:37:58.258
He's had his hand
up a couple times.

00:37:58.258 --> 00:38:01.133
AUDIENCE: Is there a limit
on the computation methods

00:38:01.133 --> 00:38:01.716
in the kernel?

00:38:01.716 --> 00:38:04.108
So can you do
distributed [INAUDIBLE]??

00:38:04.108 --> 00:38:04.900
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:07.240
So the question is, is there
a limit on the computation

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:07.990
within the kernel?

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:10.750
It has the same limits as
any other execution process.

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:14.320
So oftentimes, you're executing
when you schedule this or run

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:15.055
us on a platform.

00:38:15.055 --> 00:38:16.930
You're running usually
in some container that

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:20.080
has some resource limits,
and those are your limits.

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:23.840
It's the same as
any other process.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:24.800
Yeah?

00:38:24.800 --> 00:38:27.680
AUDIENCE: So when
you run several--

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:32.520
when you copy and save several
Jupyter runs, do you do the dif

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:35.020
and save on either parts of
the [INAUDIBLE] that change,

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:36.955
or do you save everything?

00:38:36.955 --> 00:38:38.650
MATTHEW SEAL: No,
it saves everything.

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:40.500
It's an output totally.

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:43.060
What it does when it runs is
it actually reads the IP MIB.

00:38:43.060 --> 00:38:44.380
It's a JSON schema.

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:46.810
So it loads the IP
MIB file in, verifies

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:49.570
it matches the schema
for version 4.4.

00:38:49.570 --> 00:38:51.695
Then it executes it in
memory against it, updating.

00:38:51.695 --> 00:38:53.195
And every time it
saves, it converts

00:38:53.195 --> 00:38:55.840
that in memory representation
to an output totally independent

00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:56.520
of the input.

00:38:56.520 --> 00:38:57.714
So it will actually dump it.

00:38:57.714 --> 00:38:59.214
AUDIENCE: So if I
change [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:59.214 --> 00:39:02.282
like 5% of my
notebook [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:02.282 --> 00:39:04.464
trying to experiment
with something,

00:39:04.464 --> 00:39:08.418
but it could also
save the remaining

00:39:08.418 --> 00:39:10.450
95% of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:10.450 --> 00:39:12.640
MATTHEW SEAL: Today, every
time you save, you're

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:14.207
saving the whole notebook.

00:39:14.207 --> 00:39:15.790
So that's something
that will probably

00:39:15.790 --> 00:39:19.150
change in notebook 5
format with some options

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:21.955
for being a little more
scalable in that sense.

00:39:21.955 --> 00:39:24.080
Usually, it's not a problem
unless you save really,

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:25.390
really big displays.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:28.590
Like I save a gigabyte of data,
you're going to have a bad--

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:30.395
Like if you say print
and it's a gigabyte,

00:39:30.395 --> 00:39:32.020
you're going to have
a really bad time.

00:39:32.020 --> 00:39:34.420
Of course, you'd have a bad
time in most interfaces.

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:36.545
But it's a little worse in
notebooks in that sense.

00:39:36.545 --> 00:39:39.613
But yeah, every time you save,
it saves the whole document.

00:39:39.613 --> 00:39:40.530
A question over there.

00:39:40.530 --> 00:39:42.522
AUDIENCE: So if you
will use a Python file,

00:39:42.522 --> 00:39:45.012
you have all the tools that
help you write good quality

00:39:45.012 --> 00:39:47.004
code, like [INAUDIBLE].

00:39:49.861 --> 00:39:51.486
What are you going
to use in notebooks?

00:39:51.486 --> 00:39:53.480
How do you encourage people
to write good quality code?

00:39:53.480 --> 00:39:53.730
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:55.813
I would encourage you to
bring some of those tools

00:39:55.813 --> 00:39:56.420
to notebooks.

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:57.140
There are a few.

00:39:57.140 --> 00:40:00.800
Like Linting and a lot of
UIs have some built-in stuff

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:02.138
around certain languages.

00:40:02.138 --> 00:40:03.680
Though, since it's
a polyglot system,

00:40:03.680 --> 00:40:06.350
a lot of those languages
lack nice autocompletes,

00:40:06.350 --> 00:40:08.567
nice linters, and
things like that.

00:40:08.567 --> 00:40:10.400
So if you jump over to
something like Scala,

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:11.733
it's still kind of rough.

00:40:11.733 --> 00:40:13.400
It's getting made
better because there's

00:40:13.400 --> 00:40:14.483
companies investing in it.

00:40:14.483 --> 00:40:17.480
But R studio still does
better than R in Jupyter

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:18.720
in that sense.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.970
I would say those tools
can be integrated,

00:40:20.970 --> 00:40:23.900
and I would encourage to
bring those tools to Jupyter.

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:27.583
There's a few places
where they already exist.

00:40:27.583 --> 00:40:28.250
We got a bunch--

00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:30.398
I think you were next.

00:40:30.398 --> 00:40:32.315
AUDIENCE: So I'm able
to handle the cases when

00:40:32.315 --> 00:40:34.220
the kernel actually fails.

00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:36.810
For example, to code makes
the kernel run out of memory.

00:40:36.810 --> 00:40:41.150
I'm able to save the
last execution that

00:40:41.150 --> 00:40:42.908
falls out of the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:40:42.908 --> 00:40:43.700
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:40:43.700 --> 00:40:44.658
So he's talking about--

00:40:44.658 --> 00:40:46.550
the question was,
when things go wrong,

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:49.170
like the kernel runs out
of memory, what happens,

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:50.610
or is it handled well?

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.120
Well, the nice thing is with
papermill 1.0 which released,

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:55.580
it now has a better OM handler.

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:57.260
So it'll actually
raise that it ran out

00:40:57.260 --> 00:40:59.000
of memory instead
of running forever,

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:03.230
which it used to do sometimes,
which is the worst thing it did

00:41:03.230 --> 00:41:04.580
out of things that were bad.

00:41:04.580 --> 00:41:07.550
But in terms of story arc, what
will happen when you actually

00:41:07.550 --> 00:41:09.590
run is it won't save
that final execution.

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:11.420
You'll see if you
load the notebook,

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:13.790
it'll say, executing with
papermill, dot, dot, dot.

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:16.170
And it'll be stuck
in that mode, mostly

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:18.672
because the processes die and
we don't know how to recover.

00:41:18.672 --> 00:41:20.630
But what you do get is
when you run papermill--

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:21.672
and we run it production.

00:41:21.672 --> 00:41:24.180
We run with dash-dash
log outputs.

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:26.600
And that says-- every time
we would get a message,

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:28.350
we were buffering to
save in the notebook,

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:29.550
we'd log it out right there.

00:41:29.550 --> 00:41:30.590
So we at least
have-- the container

00:41:30.590 --> 00:41:33.173
that was executing papermill has
all the logs, so if something

00:41:33.173 --> 00:41:35.060
really catastrophic
happens, we can always

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:37.477
read the logs from there, even
if the cell in the notebook

00:41:37.477 --> 00:41:38.042
failed.

00:41:38.042 --> 00:41:39.500
One thing that
would be nice, and I

00:41:39.500 --> 00:41:41.810
think we should
probably invest in

00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:43.370
that more, at least
in our platform,

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:45.715
is when it does out of
memory of making a link,

00:41:45.715 --> 00:41:47.090
like rewriting
over that notebook

00:41:47.090 --> 00:41:50.308
to add a link back to the logs
or inject the logs afterwards.

00:41:50.308 --> 00:41:52.850
It doesn't do that today, but
it would be pretty easy to add.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:57.783
Cool.

00:41:57.783 --> 00:41:59.950
AUDIENCE: Do you save it
or host in version control?

00:41:59.950 --> 00:42:01.358
And then, how do you dif it?

00:42:01.358 --> 00:42:02.150
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:04.150
So question was, do you
save in version control,

00:42:04.150 --> 00:42:05.090
and how do you dif it?

00:42:05.090 --> 00:42:07.550
The answer is I save my
stuff in version control.

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:09.890
Not everyone else does.

00:42:09.890 --> 00:42:12.020
I would say that diffing
is kind of painful.

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:13.160
It's been getting
better, actually.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:14.430
There's a few tools out there.

00:42:14.430 --> 00:42:17.608
So nbdime is a tool in
Jupyter for diffing notebooks.

00:42:17.608 --> 00:42:19.400
It's not so integrated
with Git everywhere,

00:42:19.400 --> 00:42:20.720
but there's some efforts there.

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:23.150
nbviewer is a nice--

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:27.000
it's got an open source
side and a close source side

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:28.530
for doing GitHub viewing.

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:30.110
And I encourage you to go--
if you don't use GitHub,

00:42:30.110 --> 00:42:32.652
they've got issues for other
Git systems that I've definitely

00:42:32.652 --> 00:42:34.590
plus-oned Go poke
them and tell Stash--

00:42:34.590 --> 00:42:36.920
or I mean Bitbucket
to go add an API

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:39.410
so you can do the right thing.

00:42:39.410 --> 00:42:40.700
In terms of local diffing--

00:42:40.700 --> 00:42:42.680
so the reason why I ask
this, notebook dipping

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:44.913
is really ugly because you're
diffing JSON documents.

00:42:44.913 --> 00:42:46.330
They are pretty
printed, at least,

00:42:46.330 --> 00:42:48.063
so it's not all one line.

00:42:48.063 --> 00:42:49.730
But when you want to
read it as a human,

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:51.320
you usually give up after a bit.

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:53.960
So the effort with
nbdime and other places

00:42:53.960 --> 00:42:55.998
to get integration
to your places

00:42:55.998 --> 00:42:58.040
where you do code reviews
and render the notebook

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:00.380
is a dif, a
two-sided dif, rather

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:02.240
than reading the JSON raw.

00:43:02.240 --> 00:43:04.920
Today, I read that
JSON raw sometimes,

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:07.862
and it's unfortunate.

00:43:07.862 --> 00:43:09.070
You had a follow up question.

00:43:09.070 --> 00:43:09.653
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:43:09.653 --> 00:43:13.270
So you mentioned that you save
it in the GitHub [INAUDIBLE]..

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:14.128
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:43:14.128 --> 00:43:15.670
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
company do not.

00:43:15.670 --> 00:43:17.590
So what's the reason for?

00:43:17.590 --> 00:43:20.172
MATTHEW SEAL: Some of that's
just the analysts and even

00:43:20.172 --> 00:43:21.380
some of the data scientists--

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:22.450
Git's a new thing.

00:43:22.450 --> 00:43:23.890
They maybe just learned SQL.

00:43:23.890 --> 00:43:24.820
They can do reporting.

00:43:24.820 --> 00:43:25.570
They know Tableau.

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:27.028
They know some of
those interfaces.

00:43:27.028 --> 00:43:30.412
And Git's kind of new, so it's
a scary thing to get into.

00:43:30.412 --> 00:43:31.870
And also, sometimes
people are just

00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:33.340
lazy because humans are humans.

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:36.010
So while, yes, you should always
use Git and version control,

00:43:36.010 --> 00:43:38.260
the reality is sometimes
people skip that step if they

00:43:38.260 --> 00:43:39.190
don't think it's too important.

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:40.773
And then, later, it
becomes important.

00:43:40.773 --> 00:43:42.057
They forgot to do that.

00:43:42.057 --> 00:43:43.390
What we are trying to do there--

00:43:43.390 --> 00:43:45.400
one of the tools I mentioned
there is called Bookstore.

00:43:45.400 --> 00:43:46.930
Bookstore tries to give
you linear versioning,

00:43:46.930 --> 00:43:49.120
so every time you save, it
kicks off a version to--

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:50.965
right now, it does it to S3.

00:43:50.965 --> 00:43:53.990
A blasphemy in this room, but
Google Cloud, you can plug it,

00:43:53.990 --> 00:43:55.620
make it happen.

00:43:55.620 --> 00:43:58.120
But Bookstore is a way to get
to some linear version control

00:43:58.120 --> 00:43:59.953
without having the user
have to do anything.

00:44:03.590 --> 00:44:04.090
Yeah?

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:05.380
AUDIENCE: So let's say
you have a notebook

00:44:05.380 --> 00:44:06.980
and you want to make
it into a Python file?

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:08.685
Is there an easy
way to do that or do

00:44:08.685 --> 00:44:10.390
you just have to [INAUDIBLE]?

00:44:10.390 --> 00:44:13.770
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.
nbconvert dash-dash 2 Python.

00:44:17.370 --> 00:44:17.870
Oh, sorry.

00:44:17.870 --> 00:44:20.287
The question for anyone that
was online that couldn't hear

00:44:20.287 --> 00:44:23.880
was, can you convert a notebook
into a regular Python file?

00:44:23.880 --> 00:44:27.110
And nbconvert's the
tool to do that.

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:27.900
Yeah?

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:29.317
AUDIENCE: So Jupyter
notebooks are

00:44:29.317 --> 00:44:31.200
open source, so can
anyone contribute to it,

00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:33.890
or if I wanted to contribute
to it, how would you start up

00:44:33.890 --> 00:44:34.390
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:44:34.390 --> 00:44:34.780
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:44:34.780 --> 00:44:35.633
The question was--

00:44:35.633 --> 00:44:36.550
Jupyter's open source.

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:37.930
Can anyone contribute?

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:41.230
Do we have a Donations Please
or a big Don't Enter sign?

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:42.520
No, we don't.

00:44:42.520 --> 00:44:44.800
So I'm on the
Jupyter team as well.

00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:46.420
And yes, we try
to be as friendly

00:44:46.420 --> 00:44:48.170
as we can to pull people in.

00:44:48.170 --> 00:44:50.710
There are a few of the
packages or code pass

00:44:50.710 --> 00:44:53.140
that could use some
more professional love,

00:44:53.140 --> 00:44:57.280
so we do also try and tag
issues with New User Friendly.

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:59.830
Papermill has had a lot
of brand new contributors

00:44:59.830 --> 00:45:01.380
contribute to
papermill specifically.

00:45:01.380 --> 00:45:03.880
Matter of fact, that's actually
how Google Cloud-- they just

00:45:03.880 --> 00:45:05.505
opened up a PR and
said, hey, we really

00:45:05.505 --> 00:45:07.417
love to get Google
Cloud working with this,

00:45:07.417 --> 00:45:08.500
and that went really easy.

00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:10.780
So I would say it's open,
and there's a lot of forums

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:14.830
both on Discourse, Gitter,
and the Google Group are

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:16.870
all good places to
engage if you're

00:45:16.870 --> 00:45:18.290
trying to find a place to work.

00:45:18.290 --> 00:45:20.098
And then, Interact also
has a Slack channel

00:45:20.098 --> 00:45:20.890
for lots of things.

00:45:20.890 --> 00:45:22.182
You can go ask questions there.

00:45:25.268 --> 00:45:29.435
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
as a URL parameter?

00:45:29.435 --> 00:45:30.810
MATTHEW SEAL: As
a URL parameter,

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:32.730
like go fetch them from a URL?

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:37.099
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:37.099 --> 00:45:39.391
MATTHEW SEAL: I'm not sure
I quite follow the question.

00:45:39.391 --> 00:45:42.036
AUDIENCE: I mean when I open
the [INAUDIBLE] notebook

00:45:42.036 --> 00:45:44.108
in the browser, [INAUDIBLE]
URL for that, right?

00:45:44.108 --> 00:45:44.900
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:45:44.900 --> 00:45:49.140
AUDIENCE: So can I add extra
parameters [INAUDIBLE]??

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:50.140
MATTHEW SEAL: Oh, I see.

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:53.120
So he's asking, when you
load a notebook in the UI,

00:45:53.120 --> 00:45:56.307
can you add query params to
parameterize your notebook?

00:45:56.307 --> 00:45:58.890
No, because that's not actually
running through the templating

00:45:58.890 --> 00:45:59.490
path at all.

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:01.937
That's the UI human path.

00:46:01.937 --> 00:46:04.020
There will probably be
extensions to make it easy.

00:46:04.020 --> 00:46:06.420
Some of the interfaces now
I've actually just made add

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:09.240
a notebook parameter cell
as a first-class thing

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:11.870
into the notebook interface
where you just click a button

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:15.270
and it builds a cell for
you, though traditionally,

00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:16.660
today what you have to do is--

00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:18.910
So the way it will choose
where to put your parameters

00:46:18.910 --> 00:46:23.340
and how to apply them is it will
look for a cell with the cell

00:46:23.340 --> 00:46:25.810
tag parameters, and it'll
treat that as the default.

00:46:25.810 --> 00:46:27.810
So anything in there it
thinks are the defaults,

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:28.680
and then it'll
inject after that.

00:46:28.680 --> 00:46:31.110
If it doesn't find anything,
it'll just put it at the top.

00:46:31.110 --> 00:46:33.217
But from a query
parameter point of view,

00:46:33.217 --> 00:46:35.550
it's not really an interface
you're parameterizing there

00:46:35.550 --> 00:46:37.675
because you would just
write the code cell yourself

00:46:37.675 --> 00:46:39.730
or write the default
tag cell yourself.

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:41.438
It doesn't automatically
do that for you.

00:46:43.886 --> 00:46:44.508
Cool.

00:46:44.508 --> 00:46:46.341
AUDIENCE: This is more
of a feature request.

00:46:46.341 --> 00:46:51.141
So do you have the [INAUDIBLE]
information for each cell?

00:46:51.141 --> 00:46:52.599
Because if something
real, you want

00:46:52.599 --> 00:46:54.688
to know whether it took
20 minutes [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:54.688 --> 00:46:56.170
or it took 2 and 1/2 hours.

00:46:56.170 --> 00:46:58.030
MATTHEW SEAL:
Feature request was

00:46:58.030 --> 00:47:00.873
can you add something so you
can keep track of how long cells

00:47:00.873 --> 00:47:01.540
took to execute?

00:47:01.540 --> 00:47:02.950
If you run with
papermill, it does that.

00:47:02.950 --> 00:47:03.810
AUDIENCE: Oh, it has it already?

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:04.370
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:05.037
AUDIENCE: Great.

00:47:06.744 --> 00:47:08.744
AUDIENCE: You might have
addressed this already,

00:47:08.744 --> 00:47:11.232
but if it does
fail, there's no way

00:47:11.232 --> 00:47:15.444
to start to continue execution
for it where it failed from?

00:47:15.444 --> 00:47:17.537
Suppose not failable
I'm going to say

00:47:17.537 --> 00:47:20.572
that [INAUDIBLE] interrupted.

00:47:20.572 --> 00:47:22.528
So with the metadata
from the cell,

00:47:22.528 --> 00:47:25.606
you can still see whether
it completed, whether it's

00:47:25.606 --> 00:47:26.980
pending or running.

00:47:26.980 --> 00:47:28.850
So suppose something's pending.

00:47:28.850 --> 00:47:32.770
Can you start or continue
execution where it left off?

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:36.670
MATTHEW SEAL: Basically, the
question was, if a kernel fails

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:39.490
and your papermill
execution stops,

00:47:39.490 --> 00:47:43.330
could you reattach the
kernel and run against it?

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:44.872
Today, with tools
as is, it would not

00:47:44.872 --> 00:47:46.747
do that because it shuts
down the kernel when

00:47:46.747 --> 00:47:49.270
it fails, or it attempts to,
because the kernel may already

00:47:49.270 --> 00:47:51.710
be dead.

00:47:51.710 --> 00:47:54.370
You could write some code.

00:47:54.370 --> 00:47:57.640
It'd probably take on the
order of 50, 100 lines of code

00:47:57.640 --> 00:47:59.680
to make it not do that.

00:47:59.680 --> 00:48:01.220
And actually, that
extension part

00:48:01.220 --> 00:48:02.860
where I said where you can
plug and play the I/O sync,

00:48:02.860 --> 00:48:05.440
so like FSTP, you could
awesome plug and play a thing

00:48:05.440 --> 00:48:06.100
called Engine.

00:48:06.100 --> 00:48:08.500
So it has the ability to
register custom execution

00:48:08.500 --> 00:48:09.250
engines.

00:48:09.250 --> 00:48:12.850
And you could register a custom
engine that would hold on--

00:48:12.850 --> 00:48:14.560
you could monkey
patch to overwrite

00:48:14.560 --> 00:48:17.710
the way nbconvert shuts down
the kernel and not shut it down.

00:48:17.710 --> 00:48:21.888
So maybe have an engine
called debug or interrupt,

00:48:21.888 --> 00:48:23.930
and then you could run
that version of the engine

00:48:23.930 --> 00:48:25.930
when you execute, and
then you could leave it up

00:48:25.930 --> 00:48:26.897
so you could reconnect.

00:48:26.897 --> 00:48:29.230
It would be a little complicated
from an interface point

00:48:29.230 --> 00:48:30.850
of view, but it's
physically possible,

00:48:30.850 --> 00:48:32.475
and the out-of-the-box
doesn't do that.

00:48:35.982 --> 00:48:37.440
AUDIENCE: So in
one of your slides,

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:38.962
you showed that
there are Jupyter

00:48:38.962 --> 00:48:42.399
servers [INAUDIBLE] notebooks.

00:48:42.399 --> 00:48:44.363
So who maintains those servers?

00:48:44.363 --> 00:48:47.968
Whose job is to keep those
servers up and running?

00:48:47.968 --> 00:48:48.760
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:48:48.760 --> 00:48:51.320
So the question was, in
the architecture diagram

00:48:51.320 --> 00:48:53.020
there's these Jupyter servers.

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:53.980
Who runs these servers?

00:48:53.980 --> 00:48:54.647
What do they do?

00:48:54.647 --> 00:48:56.840
Who owns them?

00:48:56.840 --> 00:48:59.140
The answer is usually a
notebooks platform team.

00:48:59.140 --> 00:49:01.400
So many places have
a notebooks team.

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:02.570
Google does here.

00:49:02.570 --> 00:49:03.520
Netflix does.

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:04.880
Most of your big vendors do.

00:49:04.880 --> 00:49:07.475
They're usually the ones who
are hosting the servers that

00:49:07.475 --> 00:49:09.100
are running everything
and figuring out

00:49:09.100 --> 00:49:11.800
with the rest of platform
how to orchestrate that,

00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:14.062
so how to isolate
resources or on demand.

00:49:14.062 --> 00:49:15.770
So there's a few tools
in the open source

00:49:15.770 --> 00:49:18.850
to help you with this, like
JupyterHub as a way of divvying

00:49:18.850 --> 00:49:19.620
out things.

00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:22.203
And I think that's a pretty
rapidly evolving story.

00:49:22.203 --> 00:49:24.370
But generally, it's usually
a notebook platform team

00:49:24.370 --> 00:49:25.550
that's managing the servers.

00:49:25.550 --> 00:49:27.640
And then, when you
click Launch My Notebook

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:29.240
or you try to load
a notebook, it'll

00:49:29.240 --> 00:49:32.430
dynamically allocate
resources for you someplace.

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:33.430
Depends on the platform.

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:34.847
Some platforms,
you have dedicated

00:49:34.847 --> 00:49:36.280
resources or shared resources.

00:49:36.280 --> 00:49:38.530
It lets you do what your
platform thinks is the right.

00:49:41.350 --> 00:49:43.510
Also, I don't know if people
are on the remote call

00:49:43.510 --> 00:49:45.640
and want to ask questions, if
that's possible, or people--

00:49:45.640 --> 00:49:46.524
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:49:46.524 --> 00:49:47.232
MATTHEW SEAL: OK.

00:49:49.600 --> 00:49:50.200
Sure.

00:49:50.200 --> 00:49:52.260
AUDIENCE: So how do
people go around building

00:49:52.260 --> 00:49:53.260
more complex pipelines?

00:49:53.260 --> 00:49:54.802
So for example,
you've got notebook A

00:49:54.802 --> 00:49:56.428
that you want to
fit into notebook B.

00:49:56.428 --> 00:49:58.470
Then you want to fan out
to a couple of notebooks

00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:02.800
that collect the results and
go to notebook B or something.

00:50:02.800 --> 00:50:05.400
So the normal API is on
the execute notebook.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:08.572
Do people [INAUDIBLE] to execute
these sort of complex things

00:50:08.572 --> 00:50:10.030
or is there some
tool [INAUDIBLE]??

00:50:10.030 --> 00:50:10.822
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:50:10.822 --> 00:50:12.430
So the papermill
and the Jupyter side

00:50:12.430 --> 00:50:14.888
basically just made it so it's
very easy for any scheduling

00:50:14.888 --> 00:50:17.290
tool that has DAG execution
to do its job well,

00:50:17.290 --> 00:50:20.060
which is doing DAGs and
linking things together.

00:50:20.060 --> 00:50:22.990
So we use an internal schedule
tool that we beefed up,

00:50:22.990 --> 00:50:24.730
and we basically
have two job types.

00:50:24.730 --> 00:50:28.710
We have run a container and
run a container with papermill.

00:50:28.710 --> 00:50:30.040
That's the two job types.

00:50:30.040 --> 00:50:31.790
But in reality, from
the user perspective,

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:34.150
we have many templates that
we default in for them.

00:50:34.150 --> 00:50:36.100
But there, we really
leave it on the tool

00:50:36.100 --> 00:50:38.860
that knows how to schedule,
not knowing what papermill is

00:50:38.860 --> 00:50:40.990
and just executing
doing that job well.

00:50:40.990 --> 00:50:42.865
So an open source example
of that is Airflow.

00:50:42.865 --> 00:50:44.260
If you have Airflow,
it's very easy

00:50:44.260 --> 00:50:45.718
to integrate
papermill and Airflow.

00:50:45.718 --> 00:50:47.038
A lot of people do it there.

00:50:47.038 --> 00:50:48.580
Luigi or some of
the other schedulers

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:50.460
can do it very easily too.

00:50:50.460 --> 00:50:53.080
But I would say in chain
of responsibilities,

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:55.660
I would let schedulers
do scheduling well

00:50:55.660 --> 00:50:57.430
and DAG executors do
DAG executing well,

00:50:57.430 --> 00:50:58.930
and papermill do
the execution well.

00:51:01.768 --> 00:51:03.580
Yeah?

00:51:03.580 --> 00:51:06.370
AUDIENCE: You had some user
personas on one of your slides,

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:08.620
like gate engineers
and stuff like that.

00:51:08.620 --> 00:51:12.555
[INAUDIBLE] the
entire [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:12.555 --> 00:51:17.080
iterating to [INAUDIBLE]
or is there hand-offs

00:51:17.080 --> 00:51:18.688
between different [INAUDIBLE]?

00:51:18.688 --> 00:51:19.480
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:51:19.480 --> 00:51:21.760
So the question was, with the
different personas we have,

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:23.470
what's the lifecycle
of a notebook with each

00:51:23.470 --> 00:51:24.220
of those personas?

00:51:24.220 --> 00:51:26.500
Are they handed
off or end-to-end?

00:51:26.500 --> 00:51:28.720
Depends more
actually on the team

00:51:28.720 --> 00:51:30.790
or how that
organization operates.

00:51:30.790 --> 00:51:35.170
So the tooling we've built spans
a bunch of orgs at Netflix.

00:51:35.170 --> 00:51:38.328
Most of the time, people
own end-to-end I would say.

00:51:38.328 --> 00:51:40.120
They iterate on the
notebook, and then they

00:51:40.120 --> 00:51:43.028
want to schedule
it, and then we ask

00:51:43.028 --> 00:51:44.320
them to follow some guidelines.

00:51:44.320 --> 00:51:47.550
Or if you want to burn yourself
and do something bad, you can.

00:51:47.550 --> 00:51:49.957
We have a hands-off
approach at Netflix,

00:51:49.957 --> 00:51:51.790
so it's a little different
than some places.

00:51:51.790 --> 00:51:54.520
I would say a few teams, and
probably more the iteration

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:56.620
cycle outside of
Netflix, is probably more

00:51:56.620 --> 00:51:59.572
the handoff model, which
is the traditional model.

00:51:59.572 --> 00:52:01.030
Hopefully, this
tooling helps lower

00:52:01.030 --> 00:52:02.738
that friction some so
the handoff is more

00:52:02.738 --> 00:52:05.560
to review and do more
like a code review

00:52:05.560 --> 00:52:08.800
and then templatizing
it rather than rewriting

00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:12.340
someone's work entirely.

00:52:12.340 --> 00:52:13.230
Yeah?

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:17.770
AUDIENCE: My notebook input be
a Google collaboratory notebook?

00:52:17.770 --> 00:52:18.520
MATTHEW SEAL: Yes.

00:52:18.520 --> 00:52:20.287
So Google Colab is a Jupyter--

00:52:20.287 --> 00:52:21.370
follows the Jupyter specs.

00:52:21.370 --> 00:52:23.662
So if you a Colab notebook,
it will run with papermill,

00:52:23.662 --> 00:52:26.470
and you can load in any other
notebook interface that's

00:52:26.470 --> 00:52:29.255
a Jupyter Notebook interface.

00:52:29.255 --> 00:52:30.880
Colab, by the way,
if you're not aware,

00:52:30.880 --> 00:52:34.090
is one of the Google-provided
notebook interfaces

00:52:34.090 --> 00:52:36.800
for managing notebooks
inside a Google Cloud--

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:41.830
or not inside Google
Cloud but inside Google.

00:52:41.830 --> 00:52:45.000
AUDIENCE: Is there a better
visualization tool or for video

00:52:45.000 --> 00:52:48.430
in notebook or [INAUDIBLE]?

00:52:48.430 --> 00:52:50.930
MATTHEW SEAL: Is there a better
visualization tool for video

00:52:50.930 --> 00:52:52.767
in notebooks?

00:52:52.767 --> 00:52:54.350
There's a few efforts
have been there.

00:52:54.350 --> 00:52:56.142
It's more about the
component that renders.

00:52:56.142 --> 00:52:59.000
So the way-- if
you actually look

00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:02.150
at what's being sent as
messages to and from the kernel,

00:53:02.150 --> 00:53:04.130
they actually-- you
send code to execute,

00:53:04.130 --> 00:53:08.330
and then asynchronously, you get
back these different messages.

00:53:08.330 --> 00:53:10.610
One of the messages
is called display,

00:53:10.610 --> 00:53:12.350
and display has
a MIME type, much

00:53:12.350 --> 00:53:14.120
like any other
front-end rendering

00:53:14.120 --> 00:53:15.780
system would understand.

00:53:15.780 --> 00:53:18.860
So then, your UI can
implement MIME type renders.

00:53:18.860 --> 00:53:21.800
So you can implement a nice--

00:53:21.800 --> 00:53:23.835
a better video
render by anything

00:53:23.835 --> 00:53:26.210
by implementing something that
responds to that MIME type

00:53:26.210 --> 00:53:28.940
and then plugging that into the
video editor player you like.

00:53:28.940 --> 00:53:30.648
So there's been some
efforts in a few UIs

00:53:30.648 --> 00:53:31.650
of making better video.

00:53:31.650 --> 00:53:34.430
Mostly, those have been closed
source extensions of Jupyter,

00:53:34.430 --> 00:53:35.740
not so much in the open source.

00:53:35.740 --> 00:53:37.490
I'm not aware of what
the current state is

00:53:37.490 --> 00:53:39.720
for open source video
rendering niceties.

00:53:39.720 --> 00:53:41.870
But if you want to
add a component that's

00:53:41.870 --> 00:53:43.880
easy for all of them
to use, I'd encourage

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:46.580
looking at the nteract
project in the nteract.

00:53:46.580 --> 00:53:48.860
That's a collection
of React components

00:53:48.860 --> 00:53:51.650
that are made to be used
in different notebook UI

00:53:51.650 --> 00:53:52.842
front ends.

00:53:52.842 --> 00:53:54.800
It's really easy to add
a new MIME type render.

00:53:57.737 --> 00:53:58.237
Yeah?

00:53:58.237 --> 00:53:58.779
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:53:58.779 --> 00:54:03.100
So I wonder how do you
guys reach to the decision

00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:07.395
that you guys want to do the
[INAUDIBLE] at a local level

00:54:07.395 --> 00:54:12.350
instead of [INAUDIBLE]
try different parameter

00:54:12.350 --> 00:54:15.618
within a notebook [INAUDIBLE]
inside a notebook?

00:54:15.618 --> 00:54:16.410
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:54:16.410 --> 00:54:18.090
So the question was
around how do we

00:54:18.090 --> 00:54:20.880
come to the decision of
branching on the notebook level

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:22.820
instead of maybe branching
within the notebook

00:54:22.820 --> 00:54:24.070
and iterating.

00:54:24.070 --> 00:54:26.480
And that actually has been--

00:54:26.480 --> 00:54:28.860
I see it as there's two
design philosophies about how

00:54:28.860 --> 00:54:30.300
notebooks would evolve.

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:33.450
One of them was the notebook
as a black box function

00:54:33.450 --> 00:54:36.655
that I'm going to execute,
or some larger system

00:54:36.655 --> 00:54:37.530
I'm going to execute.

00:54:37.530 --> 00:54:39.540
And the other was the
idea that each cell would

00:54:39.540 --> 00:54:41.250
be that functional
unit, and then you

00:54:41.250 --> 00:54:43.935
would reuse those cells somehow.

00:54:43.935 --> 00:54:45.810
Netflix ended up leaning
on the notebook side

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:47.310
because it was simpler.

00:54:47.310 --> 00:54:50.925
So the kiss it model kept
it easier to reason about.

00:54:50.925 --> 00:54:53.890
It doesn't mean the other model
is wrong or couldn't be built.

00:54:53.890 --> 00:54:55.560
But we built tools
for what we saw

00:54:55.560 --> 00:54:57.893
as the path of least resistance
that would have the most

00:54:57.893 --> 00:54:59.010
reliability.

00:54:59.010 --> 00:55:01.980
One of the issues
with trying to--

00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:03.630
one of the opinions
papermill takes

00:55:03.630 --> 00:55:06.270
is, you're going to run
this notebook linearly.

00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:08.640
Notebooks don't require you
to do that from the UI side,

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:10.110
but papermill does.

00:55:10.110 --> 00:55:11.580
And this simplifies
the problem and

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:13.570
the reproducibility
question a bit.

00:55:13.570 --> 00:55:17.110
In the other model, how
you execute your cells,

00:55:17.110 --> 00:55:19.320
it becomes almost
an acyclic graph

00:55:19.320 --> 00:55:21.120
of execution of your
different cells.

00:55:21.120 --> 00:55:22.662
And notebooks didn't
have a great way

00:55:22.662 --> 00:55:24.743
to express that DAG
within notebooks today.

00:55:24.743 --> 00:55:26.160
So if we went that
route where you

00:55:26.160 --> 00:55:28.865
want to iterate on
executing a cell,

00:55:28.865 --> 00:55:30.990
probably would have pushed
more on building tooling

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:34.977
around setting up a DAG around
inside the notebook object

00:55:34.977 --> 00:55:36.810
about how you're going
to execute your cells

00:55:36.810 --> 00:55:38.340
and how they relate better.

00:55:38.340 --> 00:55:39.840
But we didn't go
that way because we

00:55:39.840 --> 00:55:41.548
felt it was more
complicated at the time.

00:55:44.670 --> 00:55:46.510
AUDIENCE: On a
conduction standpoint,

00:55:46.510 --> 00:55:49.890
when you have a new data
scientist that comes in, how do

00:55:49.890 --> 00:55:53.170
you teach them essentially
what are the best practices

00:55:53.170 --> 00:55:54.640
for here's--

00:55:54.640 --> 00:56:00.550
and like you mentioned, a
lot of [INAUDIBLE] themselves

00:56:00.550 --> 00:56:05.940
and [INAUDIBLE] everywhere
[INAUDIBLE] access control

00:56:05.940 --> 00:56:06.948
for that or--

00:56:06.948 --> 00:56:07.740
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:56:07.740 --> 00:56:09.540
So the question was, how
do you onboard people

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:11.040
onto these best
practices, and how do you

00:56:11.040 --> 00:56:13.140
make the best practices
actually best practices

00:56:13.140 --> 00:56:16.270
instead of maybe best practices?

00:56:16.270 --> 00:56:18.688
That's really-- I can give
you the Netflix answer.

00:56:18.688 --> 00:56:20.980
I don't think it'll translate
to a lot of other places.

00:56:20.980 --> 00:56:22.630
And it's not perfect.

00:56:22.630 --> 00:56:26.400
I think, in the Netflix case,
we lean a lot on this concept

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:28.410
of freedom and responsibility.

00:56:28.410 --> 00:56:31.710
So teams, individuals are
free to do whatever they want,

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:34.243
but we expect that we've
hired experienced people that

00:56:34.243 --> 00:56:35.910
are going to make
responsible decisions.

00:56:35.910 --> 00:56:38.280
So oftentimes, within an
org, that org or that team

00:56:38.280 --> 00:56:41.040
has made decisions about
how to best onboard people.

00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:42.930
And so we try to
provide the tools

00:56:42.930 --> 00:56:45.905
and notify, inform
people that these tools

00:56:45.905 --> 00:56:47.780
and patterns and
documentation are someplace.

00:56:47.780 --> 00:56:50.220
And we leave the
responsibility on the teams

00:56:50.220 --> 00:56:52.980
to figure out which of these
they should keep track of

00:56:52.980 --> 00:56:54.190
and onboard their users with.

00:56:54.190 --> 00:56:55.690
There's some
centralized onboarding.

00:56:55.690 --> 00:56:57.960
We do training about the
scheduler and notebooks

00:56:57.960 --> 00:57:00.448
for new hires every
month and for people

00:57:00.448 --> 00:57:01.740
who just don't know what it is.

00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:03.630
So we do a little bit
of the training model

00:57:03.630 --> 00:57:04.830
and try to follow that way.

00:57:04.830 --> 00:57:06.780
It works pretty well
within our culture,

00:57:06.780 --> 00:57:08.540
but within other
cultures, you might want

00:57:08.540 --> 00:57:09.665
to do a different approach.

00:57:14.780 --> 00:57:15.410
Cool.

00:57:15.410 --> 00:57:16.580
I get to save my voice then?

00:57:19.040 --> 00:57:19.540
Awesome.

00:57:19.540 --> 00:57:20.850
I'm not sure where
we are on time.

00:57:20.850 --> 00:57:21.820
Oh, we have another question.

00:57:21.820 --> 00:57:22.386
OK.

00:57:22.386 --> 00:57:24.250
AUDIENCE: So I work
as a data scientist,

00:57:24.250 --> 00:57:28.320
and that for most
of the prototypings

00:57:28.320 --> 00:57:30.940
we worked with the
Jupyter [INAUDIBLE]..

00:57:30.940 --> 00:57:34.444
So my question is that
by using the papermill,

00:57:34.444 --> 00:57:40.200
you're trying to put your output
in a separate notebook file.

00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:41.870
What's the advantage for that?

00:57:41.870 --> 00:57:44.280
Basically, for
production, we have

00:57:44.280 --> 00:57:47.190
to somehow transfer
the notebook back

00:57:47.190 --> 00:57:51.420
to the Python script for the
efficiency and other things

00:57:51.420 --> 00:57:51.930
that--

00:57:51.930 --> 00:57:54.870
I just wonder how
or why you need

00:57:54.870 --> 00:57:57.458
to code that output
on another file.

00:57:57.458 --> 00:57:58.250
MATTHEW SEAL: Yeah.

00:57:58.250 --> 00:58:01.075
So the question was
around why separate

00:58:01.075 --> 00:58:02.700
the notebook when it
goes to production

00:58:02.700 --> 00:58:04.710
because the data
science lifecycle lives

00:58:04.710 --> 00:58:05.960
within the same notebook.

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:06.960
That's how they operate.

00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:08.793
Now I have to think
about two notebooks that

00:58:08.793 --> 00:58:10.330
exist in the wild.

00:58:10.330 --> 00:58:12.030
There's a couple
reasons we did this.

00:58:12.030 --> 00:58:16.610
One is it made data platforms
be OK with us doing this.

00:58:16.610 --> 00:58:19.650
So that's one of the-- that's
actually a really compelling

00:58:19.650 --> 00:58:22.020
thing, is it crosses a
bridge where you give this

00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:25.260
immutability guarantee about I
ran this source and it resulted

00:58:25.260 --> 00:58:26.350
in this outcome. .

00:58:26.350 --> 00:58:29.250
So from a pure execution,
independent of what it's doing,

00:58:29.250 --> 00:58:35.280
that gives a lot of nice
security and re-playability

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:37.740
and debug-ability for
people who are outside

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:39.750
of knowing what your
notebook actually did

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:41.230
or how it should operate.

00:58:41.230 --> 00:58:42.220
So that's one aspect.

00:58:42.220 --> 00:58:44.100
The other aspect
we have is that we

00:58:44.100 --> 00:58:47.610
wanted to have a very
good recollection of how

00:58:47.610 --> 00:58:50.370
your notebook changed over time
or how it iterated last time.

00:58:50.370 --> 00:58:53.490
So many times, if something
fails and a platform engineer

00:58:53.490 --> 00:58:54.960
or someone tries
to help, they have

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:57.685
to go look at what ran and
figure out why it didn't work.

00:58:57.685 --> 00:59:00.060
And knowing what the last
successful version is is really

00:59:00.060 --> 00:59:02.450
handy because you can go look
at the last successful run,

00:59:02.450 --> 00:59:03.600
compare it with the current run.

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:05.392
That can tell you a
lot of things about why

00:59:05.392 --> 00:59:06.630
something isn't working.

00:59:06.630 --> 00:59:09.315
Now, how does that feed
back in as a model?

00:59:09.315 --> 00:59:11.535
Or how do you have a template?

00:59:11.535 --> 00:59:13.410
One thing that happens
as a development side,

00:59:13.410 --> 00:59:15.540
if you schedule something with
a particular parameterization,

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:17.310
usually, when you want
to try playing with it,

00:59:17.310 --> 00:59:18.810
you want that same
parameterization.

00:59:18.810 --> 00:59:21.690
So many times, people in
the data science cycle

00:59:21.690 --> 00:59:25.275
would schedule a notebook, run
it, and they go see how it ran.

00:59:25.275 --> 00:59:27.900
And either they'll just copy the
parameter cell back and run it

00:59:27.900 --> 00:59:30.000
because it's the same
source, or they'll

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:31.110
copy the whole notebook.

00:59:31.110 --> 00:59:32.610
And then, when
they're done, they'll

00:59:32.610 --> 00:59:34.470
just delete the parameter
cell and save it.

00:59:34.470 --> 00:59:38.973
So there in the cycle is
a little bit manual still.

00:59:38.973 --> 00:59:40.890
There could be tooling
to make it less manual.

00:59:40.890 --> 00:59:43.050
And I think that's where a
lot of the tooling is going,

00:59:43.050 --> 00:59:44.410
is smoothing over these edges.

00:59:44.410 --> 00:59:46.410
It's a little bit of a
bump for a really big win

00:59:46.410 --> 00:59:50.100
to be cooperative with
the rest of the ecosystem.

00:59:50.100 --> 00:59:52.138
Does that help
answer the question?

00:59:52.138 --> 00:59:52.638
OK.

00:59:57.880 --> 00:59:58.410
Awesome.

00:59:58.410 --> 01:00:00.010
Honestly, my voice--
and I'll be around,

01:00:00.010 --> 01:00:01.218
so maybe I'll lose it anyway.

01:00:01.218 --> 01:00:03.870
But thank you everyone,
and I hope you enjoyed.

01:00:03.870 --> 01:00:05.070
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:05.070 --> 01:00:07.520
